{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013zsrWgQAkfn6oULpszza1c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand what needs to be done.\n\n## Project Summary\n\nThis is a Go reimplementation of GNU coreutils/moreutils, verified via differential testing. The pipeline is: extract specs from C source → specify (SRD + use cases) → synthesize (AI generates Go) → verify (differential testing).\n\n## Current State\n\nLooking at the code_status, ALL use cases across ALL releases have `codestatus: not started`, except:\n- rel00.0-uc003-sys: implemented\n- rel00.0-uc004-testutils: implemented\n\n\nSo pkg/sys and pkg/testutils already exist, which means I need to build everything else. The requirement_states show all requirements are marked ready, indicating nothing's been completed in this generation run yet.\n\n## Priority\n\nFollowing P1 (release-driven priority), the earliest incomplete release is **00.0** (Batch 0 — foundational infrastructure). It has rel00.0-uc001-magefiles and rel00.0-uc002-format both spec-complete but not started, while rel00.0-uc003-sys is already implemented.\n\nFor rel00.0, I need to build magefiles, format, hashutil, sizeparse, and encutil—sys and testutils are already done. But there's a tension here: the architecture says magefiles should be provided by cobbler-scaffold's orchestrator library, and design decision 7 says the generation pipeline shouldn't target magefiles. Yet srd011-magefiles has all requirements marked ready, which seems to contradict that architectural constraint.\n\nLooking closer at the architecture note, only cmd/version within magefiles needs to be generated, and srd059-version requirements are all ready too. Now I need to figure out what to propose, which requires checking weight budgets from requirements.yaml—but I can't access that file and I'm constrained from using tools to read it. I'll have to work with the assumption that requirements default to weight 1 unless specified otherwise.\n\nFor the package contracts, I can see that srd001-testutils and srd002-sys are already implemented, while srd003-format, srd086-hashutil, srd087-sizeparse, and srd088-encutil still need work. Since I'm limited to 4 tasks and format is the only one with a dependency (on sys, which is done), I should prioritize format along with the others that have no blocking dependencies.\n\nLooking at the requirement counts across these packages, format has the most work at 17 requirements, while hashutil, sizeparse, and encutil each have around 8-9 requirements. Given the constraint of 4 tasks maximum, I need to decide which combination makes the most sense for this release. I'm seeing that the remaining requirements for format and hashutil are more complex, so I'll prioritize the simpler packages first. My plan is to tackle sizeparse and encutil across four tasks, each handling up to four requirements, which keeps everything manageable within the constraint. Wait, but the constraint says \"Always split implementation and tests into separate tasks.\" So I shouldn't mix implementation and tests. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the SRD requirement groups:\n- srd087-sizeparse: R1.x, R2.x, R3.x groups\n- R3.x appears to be error handling / edge cases, not tests\n\nThe test files would be separate. Let me think about what makes sense for the stitch agent.\n\nFor sizeparse, I can do:\n- Task 0: pkg/sizeparse implementation (R1.1-R1.4) - core parsing\n- Task 1: pkg/sizeparse implementation (R2.1-R2.2, R3.1-R3.2) - options and errors\n\nFor encutil:\n- Task 2: pkg/encutil implementation (R1.1-R1.3, R2.1) - encoding\n- Task 3: pkg/encutil implementation (R2.2-R2.3, R3.1-R3.2) - decoding and input\n\nNow I need to look at the actual contracts more carefully. The sizeparse contract defines Parse, ParseWithOptions, and ParseOptions, while encutil provides EncoderConfig, DecoderConfig, Encode, Decode, and OpenInput. These are fairly compact packages, so I should structure the tasks around implementing these specific functions. For sizeparse, I can split the eight requirements into two tasks of four each, and do the same for encutil's eight requirements. = weight 4 ✓\n\nThis gives exactly 4 tasks. Good.\n\nBut wait - I need to read the SRDs to understand what R1.1 etc actually mean for each package. I don't have the SRD content inline. The project_context only has requirement_states (ready/complete status), not requirement text.\n\nI'll need to infer from the contracts what each group likely covers:\n\nsrd087-sizeparse:\n- R1.x: Core parsing (Parse function, basic size suffixes)\n- R2.x: Options (ParseWithOptions, AllowSign, DefaultUnit)\n- R3.x: Error handling\n\nsrd088-encutil:\n- R1.x: Encoding (EncoderConfig, Encode, wrap)\n- R2.x: Decoding (DecoderConfig, Decode, ignore garbage)\n- R3.x: Input handling (OpenInput)\n\nNow I'm drafting the tasks with required_reading pointing to the SRD files, file modifications with create/modify actions, requirements anchored to SRD IDs, design decisions from the contracts, and acceptance criteria. For file naming, I need to follow the convention that Go packages name their primary source file after the primary exported type - for sizeparse, that's the Parse function, so parse.go makes sense, and for encutil I'll use similar logic based on the primary exports. I'm second-guessing the file naming—codec.go might be better since both encoding and decoding live there. But I should stick to what the SRD actually specifies rather than inventing design choices. Let me move forward with drafting the tasks, making sure each one references the relevant requirements and dependencies from the architecture.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":60366,"cache_read_input_tokens":11311,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_token